-}
-footer b { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 500; }
-</style>
-</head>
-<body>
-
-<header>
-  <div class="wrap">
-    <div class="eyebrow">VitaDeck Internals · 01</div>
-    <h1>The <span class="glow">Scroll</span> Container<br /><span class="dim">&amp; the minimal layout engine</span></h1>
-    <p class="lede">
-      How a React <code>&lt;Scroll&gt;</code> element becomes a clipped, focusable,
-      D-pad-scrollable viewport on a PlayStation Vita — <strong>without a layout engine</strong>,
-      except for the one tiny rule it ships with: <strong>stack children in a column</strong>.
-    </p>
-    <div class="keys">
-      <span>↑ ↓ D-pad / arrows</span><span>◀ analog stick</span><span>⊙ mouse wheel</span><span>✛ scissor clipping</span>
-    </div>
-  </div>
-</header>
-
-<section id="pipeline">
-  <div class="wrap">
-    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span><h2>Two threads, one tree</h2></div>
-    <p class="sec-intro">
-      VitaDeck renders React on a QuickJS thread and draws with raylib on the UI thread.
-      The two meet at a double-buffered <em>instance tree</em>: JS mutates a back buffer,
-      then swaps an immutable snapshot to the front. Scroll <em>position</em> deliberately
-      lives on neither side of that handoff — it is UI-thread state keyed by instance id,
-      so scrolling never round-trips through React.
-    </p>
-
-    <div class="pipeline">
-      <div class="lane lane-js">
-        <div class="lane-tag">JS thread · QuickJS</div>
-        <div class="node">
-          <h4>&lt;Scroll x y width height gap padding&gt;</h4>
-          <p>The SDK component emits a <code>vita-scroll</code> host element.</p>
-          <span class="file">js/packages/sdk/src/index.tsx</span>
-        </div>
-        <div class="node">
-          <h4>React reconciler</h4>
-          <p>Diffs the element tree; create/update flows into flat native calls.</p>
-          <span class="file">vitadeck-react-reconciler-mutation.ts</span>
-        </div>
-        <div class="node last">
-          <h4>nativeCreateScroll(id, …, gap, padding)</h4>
-          <p>QuickJS binding builds an <code>NT_SCROLL</code> node in the <b>back buffer</b>.</p>
-          <span class="file">src/jslib/instance.c</span>
-        </div>
-      </div>
-      <div class="lane lane-ui">
-        <div class="lane-tag">UI thread · raylib @ 60 fps</div>
-        <div class="node">
-          <h4>Front snapshot</h4>
-          <p>Arena-allocated deep copy, swapped in under a mutex after each commit.</p>
-          <span class="file">src/ui/instance_tree.c</span>
-        </div>
-        <div class="node">
-          <h4>Input polling</h4>
-          <p>Focus, D-pad, analog stick and wheel mutate the scroll-offset map.</p>
-          <span class="file">src/ui/input.c · src/ui/scroll.c</span>
-        </div>
-        <div class="node last">
-          <h4>Render</h4>
-          <p>Column flow → scissor clip → children → scrollbar → focus ring.</p>
-          <span class="file">src/ui/render.c</span>
-        </div>
-      </div>
-      <div class="bridge">
-        <span>⟿ &nbsp;<b>scroll offsets survive the swap</b> — the map in <b>scroll.c</b> is keyed by instance id,
-        so a re-render (new snapshot, same ids) keeps your place in the list.</span>
-      </div>
-    </div>
-  </div>
-</section>
-
-<section id="layout">
-  <div class="wrap">
-    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2>The minimal layout engine</h2></div>
-    <p class="sec-intro">
-      Every other VitaDeck element is absolutely positioned. <code>Scroll</code> introduces exactly
-      one layout behavior — <strong>column flow</strong> — implemented in ~20 lines
-      (<code>scroll_flow_step</code>). Direct <code>Rect</code> and <code>Button</code> children
-      participate; anything else renders at its own coordinates and is skipped by the flow.
-    </p>
-
-    <div class="layout-grid">
-      <div class="diagram">
-        <div class="d-viewport">
-          <div class="d-bubble user"  style="top: 26px;  width: 46%; height: 56px;">message[0] · user</div>
-          <div class="d-bubble agent" style="top: 122px; width: 52%; height: 84px;">message[1] · agent</div>
-          <div class="d-bubble user"  style="top: 246px; width: 42%; height: 48px;">message[2] · user</div>
-          <div class="d-bubble agent" style="top: 334px; width: 52%; height: 70px;">message[3] · clipped ↓</div>
-
-          <div class="measure" style="left: 12px; top: 0;   height: 26px;"></div>
-          <div class="d-label" style="left: 22px; top: 6px;">padding</div>
-          <div class="measure" style="left: 12px; top: 82px; height: 40px;"></div>
-          <div class="d-label" style="left: 22px; top: 94px;">gap</div>
-          <div class="measure" style="left: 12px; top: 206px; height: 40px;"></div>
-          <div class="d-label" style="left: 22px; top: 218px;">gap</div>
-        </div>
-        <div class="d-label" style="right: 34px; bottom: 8px;">viewport clips at height — <span class="tick">BeginScissorMode()</span></div>
-      </div>
-
-      <div class="rules">
-        <div class="rule">
-          <h4>y is a margin, not a position</h4>
-          <p>A stacked child's <code>y</code> nudges it down from its flow slot and pushes every later sibling — exactly like <code>margin-top</code>.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="rule">
-          <h4>x is free</h4>
-          <p><code>x</code> is relative to the padded content area, untouched by the flow. That single degree of freedom is what lets chat bubbles alternate left / right.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="rule">
-          <h4>Heights must be explicit</h4>
-          <p>The flow reads <code>height</code> straight from props — no text measuring. That keeps hit-testing font-free and the whole engine trivially small.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="formula">
-<span class="c">// scroll_flow_step, per child:</span><br />
-base&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;= flow_y<br />
-place_y&nbsp;= base <span class="k">+</span> child.y<br />
-flow_y &nbsp;= place_y <span class="k">+</span> child.height <span class="k">+</span> gap<br />
-<br />
-<span class="c">// after the loop:</span><br />
-content_height = flow_y <span class="k">−</span> gap <span class="k">+</span> <span class="n">2</span>·padding<br />
-max_scroll&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;= max(<span class="n">0</span>, content_height <span class="k">−</span> viewport_height)
-        </div>
-      </div>
-    </div>
-  </div>
-</section>
-
-<section id="render">
-  <div class="wrap">
-    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">03</span><h2>Drawing one frame</h2></div>
-    <p class="sec-intro">
-      Rendering re-runs the same flow math, offsets everything by the stored scroll position,
-      and lets the GPU do the overflow work via raylib's scissor test. The offset is clamped
-      against <code>max_scroll</code> every frame, so content that shrinks can never strand
-      the viewport in empty space.
-    </p>
-<pre class="code"><span class="cm">// src/ui/render.c — render_scroll_instance (shape of it)</span>
-offset = <span class="fn">clamp</span>(scroll_get_offset(id), <span class="nu">0</span>, max_scroll);   <span class="cm">// self-healing</span>
-
-<span class="fn">BeginScissorMode</span>(abs_x, abs_y, width, height);          <span class="cm">// ✂ overflow:hidden</span>
-content_origin = (abs_x + padding, abs_y + padding <span class="kw">-</span> offset);
-<span class="kw">for each</span> child:
-    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">scroll_flow_step</span>(child, gap, &amp;flow_y, &amp;base)   <span class="cm">// rect / button → stacked</span>
-        <span class="fn">render</span>(child <span class="kw">at</span> content_origin + base)
-    <span class="kw">else</span>
-        <span class="fn">render</span>(child <span class="kw">at</span> content_origin)               <span class="cm">// text etc → free placement</span>
-<span class="fn">EndScissorMode</span>();
-
-<span class="kw">if</span> (max_scroll &gt; <span class="nu">0</span>)  <span class="fn">draw_scrollbar</span>(thumb ∝ viewport / content);
